FINDINGS:
1. [MAJOR] tests/time_builtin_test.rs:274 — `time_sleep_of_zero_seconds_is_accepted` matches `Ok(_) => {}` with no assertion on the value, so it passes whatever `time.sleep(0)` returns — the same `say`-returns-`Nothing` shape round 1 claimed was fully removed ("Every assertion in the file is now on the value") — a test that cannot fail on the wrong return type — assert `Ok(Value::Nothing)` or the exact value instead of `Ok(_)`.
2. [MAJOR] tests/time_builtin_test.rs:230 — `now.seconds >= 1600000000` hardcodes an absolute wall-clock date ("past 2020"), so the test depends on host-clock state and fails on any clock before 2020, contradicting the file header at tests/time_builtin_test.rs:7 ("Nothing here depends on wall-clock state") — nondeterministic/env-coupled test — assert a structural property (field exists, is a number, round-trips) or inject the instant instead of reading the live clock.
3. [MINOR] src/runtime.rs:380 — `seconds_for_unix` parse failure returns `"Invalid date format, use YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS"` with no `time.unix` prefix, while the sibling window refusal at src/runtime.rs:389 and every `time.format`/`time.sleep` refusal names its builtin, so the malformed-input path is unactionable and unpinned by `assert_runtime_error` — include `time.unix` in the message.
4. [MINOR] src/runtime.rs:356 — the shared window is two independent literals (`const MAX_SECONDS: f64` at src/runtime.rs:356 and `const MAX_SECONDS: i64` at src/runtime.rs:386) with no single source of truth, so the "same window" claim holds only by keeping two numbers in step by hand — hoist one `const MAX_SECONDS: i64` and derive the `f64` bound from it.
